## 1. New regression test (the missing deliverable)

Added `testFirstHopFailureReturnsTypedJumpErrorWithoutTeardownCrash` to
`BicTermCore/Tests/BicTermCoreTests/ProxyJumpTests.swift` (placed next to its
structural twin `testSecondHopFailureClosesPriorHopAndNamesFailingHost`).

Design:
- REAL failure, not a mock: jump chain hop 1 dials 127.0.0.1:1 where nothing
  listens (verified pre-run: `lsof -nP -iTCP:1 -sTCP:LISTEN` empty; fixture
  sshds bind only 12222/12223). NIOJumpDialer's TCP connect fails, its catch
  shuts down its private MultiThreadedEventLoopGroup, and it throws
  `.unreachable`; JumpChainBuilder.buildChained maps it to
  `JumpError.hopFailed(hopIndex: 1, host: "127.0.0.1", port: 1,
  underlying: .unreachable)` — asserted with exact equality.
- Teardown-without-crash: the failure hits buildChained's zero-established-hops
  branch; `established` stays empty (append happens only after a successful
  dial), so the reverse-order close loop is a no-op by construction. The test
  completing plus the rest of the class (incl. the live two-hop round trip)
  passing in the same process is the no-crash/no-leak proof.
- Chain-abort assertion: `RecordingKeyProvider` saw exactly
  `[fixture-ed25519]` — key resolution precedes the dial (NIOJumpDialer.
  connectTCP calls makeUserAuthDelegate before bootstrap.connect), and the
  destination credential was never consulted.
- Ordering: the test touches neither fixture port, so it cannot perturb the
  PerSourcePenalty cadence between the hop2 auth tests (documented in-file).

## 2. Teardown audit — existing implementation already index-safe (no source change)

Audited `BicTermCore/Sources/BicTermCore/SSH/Jump/JumpChainBuilder.swift`
(buildChained), `Jump/JumpPipeline.swift` (NIOJumpDialer, NIOJumpHopConnection,
NIOJumpRawLink, NIOJumpSession), `Jump/JumpTransport.swift`,
`Jump/JumpTerminalTransport.swift`, and `SSHTransport.tearDown()`. Findings:

- Every failure path closes established hops by ITERATING
  `established.reversed()` — array iteration, no manual index arithmetic into
  `established`; an empty array yields zero iterations (no `Index out of
  range` possible, satisfying the plan's "guard empty arrays" requirement).
- Failed candidates never enter `established`: append occurs strictly after
  `connectTCP`/`connectNested` succeed, so no partially-built hop is leaked
  and none is closed twice.
- The one indexed access, `endpoints[failingIndex - 1]`, is bounded by
  construction: `attributedHopIndex` returns either `ownerIndex` (the loop
  index, in `1..<endpoints.count`) or `targetIndex` (index + 1, <= count).
- connectNested failure closes the direct-tcpip `link` channel, then the
  established hops — distinct channels; NIO `channel.close()` is idempotent,
  so closing the link and later its owner connection is not a double-close.
- First-hop TCP failure: the dialer itself shuts down its private
  EventLoopGroup before throwing (JumpPipeline.swift connectTCP catch).
- JumpTransport.close() and JumpTerminalTransport.close() are `isClosed`
  guarded — single teardown, idempotent.
- SSHTransport.tearDown() nils each stored property before awaiting its
  close — safe under reentrancy.

Conclusion: the hardening the plan asked for is already satisfied by the
committed implementation; per task instructions this is DOCUMENTED rather
than re-implemented. No production source was modified (files changed:
ProxyJumpTests.swift only).

